/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::setupPhysicsBody(number mass, number momentOfInertia)
\brief Set up the optional physics body with the specified mass and inertia.

Every call applies both arguments, including their defaults: setupPhysicsBody() sets mass and moment of inertia to 1, and setupPhysicsBody(5) sets mass to 5 and inertia to 1. An existing body keeps its identity, transform, velocity, mode, forces, constraints and other settings. Read .physics to access it without reconfiguration. Invalid mass or inertia throws before changing either value.

If absent, creates a dynamic body at the owner's current transform and stops programmed movement/spin. Removal detaches retained references; JavaScript keeps them alive automatically.

\param mass Finite positive mass; default 1.
\param momentOfInertia Finite positive moment of inertia; default 1.

\return The owner's PhysicsBody. The read-only physics property refers to this same object.

\note Dynamic bodies own movement and rotation. Programmed movement/spin requests on their owners throw; immediate setters teleport the body.

\par Example
\code{.js}
const body = sprite.setupPhysicsBody(2, 4);
body.applyImpulse(new pdg.Vector(4, 0));
\endcode

\see removePhysicsBody(), PhysicsBody
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::removePhysicsBody()
\brief Detach the optional physics body.

Restores the physics property to PhysicsBody.NoPhysics. A retained body keeps its state, loses its owner/solver, and can be advanced with PhysicsBody.step(). Calling when no body exists is harmless.

\return No value.

\see setupPhysicsBody(), PhysicsBody::step()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::createPart(string name)
\brief Create an independently animated Part.

The Sprite owns and advances the Part. Names must be nonempty and unique within this Sprite; invalid names throw. Parts do not require Spriter artwork or physics.

\param name Nonempty name unique within the owning Sprite.

\return The new Part. Its ID remains stable while owned by this Sprite; transferPart() assigns a new destination-local ID.

\par Example
\code{.js}
const hand = sprite.createPart('hand');
hand.moveTo(new pdg.Point(12, 0), 0.25, pdg.easeInOutQuad);
\endcode

\see findPart(), getPart(), removePart()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::getPart(uint id)
\brief Find an owned Part by ID.

\param id ID returned by Part.getId(), scoped to this Sprite.

\return The matching Part, or null if the ID is absent.

\see findPart(), getPartNames()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::findPart(string name)
\brief Find an owned Part by name.

\param name The exact name passed to createPart().

\return The matching Part, or null if no Part has that name.

\see createPart(), getPart()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::getPartCount()
\brief Count owned Parts.

\return The number of Parts currently owned by this Sprite, including attachment mounts.

\see getPartNames(), clearParts()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::getPartNames()
\brief List owned Part names.

\return A new array of strings in Part creation order. Editing it does not rename Parts.

\see findPart(), getPartCount()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::removePart(uint id)
\brief Remove an owned Part.

Detaches any mounted Sprites in this Part's descendant chain. Direct child Parts stay owned by the Sprite and lose this explicit parent. Retained references to the removed Part remain valid, keep local state, and stop automatic updates; its bindings and physics body are detached. Removing a mapped physical-rig Part requires disableAnimationPhysics() first; otherwise this operation throws without changing the graph.

\param id The Part ID, scoped to this Sprite.

\return True if the Part existed and was removed; false for an absent ID.

\see Part::isAttached(), clearParts()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::clearParts()
\brief Remove all owned Parts.

Detaches mounts, authored bindings, and physics bodies. Retained Part references remain usable with their local state, but no longer update with the Sprite. If any Part belongs to the active physical rig, throws before removing anything; call disableAnimationPhysics() first.

\return No value.

\see removePart(), Part::isAttached()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::getAttachmentPart()
\brief Find the mounting Part controlling this Sprite.

\return The mounting Part, or null when this Sprite is not attached through a Part. Animate the returned Part to change the attachment offset.

\see Part::attachSprite(), Part::detachSprite()
*/
